[ system ]
{# ── SEEDING, READ FROM THE RECORD RATHER THAN GUESSED FROM A NUMBER ─────────
   Replaces rapport as the primary seeding signal. Measured 2026-09-01 across 63
   seeded followers: rapport was exactly 5.0 for 57% of them and <= 10 for 76%,
   and rank was 3 for 61 of 63. Both inputs were near-constant, so the seed was
   ~200 for almost everyone regardless of what had actually happened between
   them. SeedTarget's own docstring claimed rapport was "a persisted number
   expressing how she actually feels, earned in real play" - it was not being
   earned at all.

   The original design called for an LLM read of the diary and was talked out of
   it on the grounds that "deterministic beats judged, when the deterministic
   thing is measuring the right quantity". The measurement was never checked and
   it was not measuring the quantity. This is that read, restored, and the
   material is far richer now than when it was dropped - 4,211 memories and 368
   diary entries in the store on the development save.

   RANK AND RAPPORT SURVIVE AS A FLOOR, never as a ceiling: a cautious read must
   not be able to lower someone a high rapport already justifies. They were only
   ever meant to be one of many inputs rather than the primary one. #}
{% set npc_uuid = formid_to_uuid(npc_formid) %}
You are judging how far along a relationship ALREADY IS, from the record of what has happened. You are not deciding what should happen next and you are not scoring a conversation.

{{ npc_name }} has just come under observation. Everything below already happened, before anyone was watching. Your one job is to say where they actually stand with {{ player.name }} today, so the record starts from the truth instead of from zero.

WHAT YOU ARE CHOOSING BETWEEN

- `STRANGER` - they have met. Nothing has passed between them that either would remember.
- `ACQUAINTANCE` - known to each other, on ordinary terms. Shared errands, shared road, no weight.
- `FRIEND` - they matter to each other. Trust, easy company, and something either would defend.
- `CONFIDANT` - deep and mutual, the person you would call first. Real weight, and more than friendship, but you could still describe it without using the word love.
- `DEVOTED` - a love affair in everything but the saying of it. BOTH of them behave as partners, or as good as married, and the record shows it as a shared and settled fact rather than as something either of them feels or hopes for.

THE TOP TWO ARE A REAL CHOICE, so make it. `CONFIDANT` is the honest answer for a bond that is deep. `DEVOTED` is for one where being together is already an established fact of both their lives.

ONE-SIDED LONGING IS NOT `DEVOTED`, however intense it is. Someone wanting {{ player.name }}, aching for them, calling it a consuming desire, or hoping it becomes something one day is describing THEIR OWN FEELINGS - and feelings that strong are exactly what a relationship on its way somewhere looks like. That is `FRIEND`, or `CONFIDANT` if the closeness is real and mutual. It is not the top of this scale.

THE TEST FOR `DEVOTED` IS WHETHER YOU CAN NAME WHAT THEY BOTH DID. "She is his First Wife and the mother of his children" is a shared, settled fact and passes. "She hopes in time to bridge the gap between her faith and her desire for him" is one person's inner life, hedged, and does not - no matter how moving it reads.

AND HEDGED LANGUAGE DISQUALIFIES IT. "Perhaps", "in time", "one day", "if he ever" - anything written in the conditional is a relationship that has not arrived. Score where they ARE.

THERE IS NOTHING ABOVE `DEVOTED` HERE, and that is deliberate rather than a judgement about them. Whether two people become lovers is a question {{ player.name }} has to answer out loud, and it is not yours to answer on their behalf by handing it over as a starting position. `DEVOTED` places them one conversation away from being asked, which is exactly where overwhelming evidence should leave them.

READ THE RECORD, NOT THE ROLE. A housecarl sworn to protect {{ player.name }} is doing her job; that is not intimacy. A follower who has travelled a thousand miles has logged distance, not closeness. What counts is what the two of them have actually said and done to each other.

AND BELIEVE THE RECORD WHEN IT STATES A FACT. If a diary entry or a memory says in plain words that they are lovers, that they share a bed or a home or a child, that they married - believe it, and do not discount it for being plainly worded. This mod's usual caution runs the other way and guards against inventing feeling that is not there; here the opposite failure has also happened, with recorded, mutual devotion read as ordinary companionship. But a FACT about the relationship is what earns this. An intensity of feeling, however vividly written, is not the same thing and must not be read as one.

{% if npc_married == 1 %}
{{ npc_name }} and {{ player.name }} ARE MARRIED. This is recorded, not claimed. Answer `DEVOTED` unless the record contradicts the marriage itself.
{% endif %}

[ user ]
WHO THEY ARE
{% set profile = "" %}
{% if npc_uuid and npc_uuid != 0 %}
{# NOT render_character_profile("transform"). Copied verbatim from
   snrom_author_disposition, which refuses transform for exactly this reason and
   says so at length. I used transform here anyway and it produced the failure
   below, measured 2026-09-01 on Olfina Gray-Mane:

   The judge was asked how devoted she is and handed, in order, twelve
   SeverActions kink blocks, the PLAYER'S ENTIRE INVENTORY - 51 armour pieces,
   60 potions, 84 ingredients, 91 books, 40 keys - her gold, her job, her hunger,
   and the line "You have only recently begun traveling with Haruk." It answered
   DEVOTED and moved her 229 -> 1999.

   The six bio_* modes each resolve to exactly one submodule, which is what keeps
   curated character content in and item lists out. #}
{% set profile = render_character_profile("bio_summary", npc_uuid) + render_character_profile("bio_background", npc_uuid) + render_character_profile("bio_personality", npc_uuid) + render_character_profile("bio_aspirations", npc_uuid) + render_character_profile("bio_occupation", npc_uuid) + render_character_profile("bio_relationships", npc_uuid) %}
{% endif %}
{% if profile and profile != "" %}
{{ profile }}
{% else %}
{{ npc_bio }}
{% endif %}

{# BOTH OF THESE RETURN LISTS, NOT STRINGS, and testing them with != "" is why
   neither section rendered on the first three reads. The judge was asked to
   weigh a relationship and given no diary and no memories at all - measured
   2026-09-01. Tested with length() and iterated, copied verbatim from
   snrom_author_disposition rather than paraphrased, which is what I should have
   done the first time. #}
{% set diaries = get_diary_entries(npc_uuid, 8) %}
{% if diaries and length(diaries) > 0 %}
WHAT {{ npc_name }} WRITES WHEN NO ONE IS READING

Their own private account, and the most reliable thing here. Read it before anything else and let it settle any question the rest of this leaves open.
{% for e in diaries %}
[{{ e.entry_date_str }}] {{ e.content }}{% if e.emotion %} ({{ e.emotion }}){% endif %}
{% endfor %}
{% endif %}

{% set mem = get_relevant_memories(npc_uuid, 8) %}
{% if length(mem) > 0 %}
WHAT {{ npc_name }} REMEMBERS
{% for m in mem %}
- {{ m.memory.content }}{% if m.memory.emotion %} ({{ m.memory.emotion }}){% endif %}
{% endfor %}
{% endif %}

{% if npc_prior and npc_prior != "" %}
WHAT OTHER SYSTEMS ALREADY RECORD
{{ npc_prior }}
{% endif %}

Answer in exactly this form and nothing else:

NAME: {{ npc_name }}
STANDING: <STRANGER|ACQUAINTANCE|FRIEND|CONFIDANT|DEVOTED>
BECAUSE: <one sentence naming the specific thing in the record that decided it - a diary line, a remembered moment. Not a summary of their personalities.>
